/**
 * Embedded Inter font data (OFL-1.1, see INTER-OFL-LICENSE.txt in this
 * directory) -- Regular (400) and Bold (700) weights only, raw TrueType
 * (TTF/sfnt) format. Source: @fontsource/inter's WOFF files, converted to
 * raw TTF via Python fonttools (`font.flavor = None; font.save(...)`),
 * then SUBSET via `fonttools subset` to a broad Latin + punctuation range
 * (U+0020-007E, U+00A0-017F, U+2010-2027, U+2039-203A, U+20B9) with the
 * GSUB/GPOS/GDEF tables and all OpenType-layout features dropped entirely.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT'S RAW TTF BYTES FED TO opentype.js RATHER
 * THAN A CSS @font-face: real bug found via the Final Production Loop
 * brief's Step 5 E2E staging validation -- a real generated composite came
 * back on Vercel's actual serverless runtime with every shape (bands,
 * pills, accent lines, icons) rendered correctly but EVERY on-image
 * character completely invisible, while the identical SVG rendered
 * perfectly on a local dev machine. Root cause: that container's SVG
 * rasterizer (sharp -> libvips -> librsvg, via Pango/fontconfig) can't
 * reliably shape or rasterize `<text>` glyphs at all in a fresh serverless
 * environment. A first fix attempt embedded font bytes as a base64
 * `@font-face` in the SVG's own `<style>` block -- tried as both WOFF and
 * raw TTF -- which measurably progressed the failure (real layout/kerning
 * started happening) but still rendered every glyph as a tofu box
 * (.notdef), meaning the host's text-SHAPING layer itself isn't reliably
 * available, independent of whether the font bytes are present.
 *
 * The fix that actually works (confirmed via a real E2E generation against
 * the live staging tenant): don't ask the host to shape/rasterize text at
 * all. text-overlay-render.ts uses opentype.js to parse these bytes and
 * compute each line's real glyph OUTLINES as SVG path data in this
 * module's own JS, ahead of time -- the rasterizer then just fills vector
 * paths, exactly like every rect/circle/line elsewhere in that file. No
 * font lookup, no text shaping, no fontconfig dependency at render time.
 *
 * WHY SUBSET WITH GSUB/GPOS/GDEF DROPPED: a second real bug, found
 * immediately after switching to glyph-path rendering -- opentype.js's own
 * OpenType-layout engine doesn't support every GSUB lookup type Inter's
 * full font actually uses (a ccmp/glyph-composition lookup specifically),
 * and throws unconditionally rather than skipping the unsupported feature
 * (there is no public option to suppress it). None of these features
 * (ligatures, contextual alternates, glyph composition) matter for plain
 * marketing captions -- stripping the tables that trigger them removes the
 * crash at its source instead of working around it per call site.
 */

export const INTER_REGULAR_TTF_BASE64 = "";
export const INTER_BOLD_TTF_BASE64 = "";
